1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,893
Imagine showing a robot how to do a task,
just once. What should it remember?

2
00:00:05,033 --> 00:00:11,220
The demonstration matters, of course. But so does
everything that happens after the robot starts moving.

3
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:18,037
That's the idea behind RoboDemo: remember the intended
procedure, and track the progress actually made.

4
00:00:18,908 --> 00:00:24,882
A short instruction might name the goal without explaining
the grasp, the movement, or the order of the steps.

5
00:00:25,022 --> 00:00:31,379
A video fills in some of those details. But here's the
catch: it can't tell us what the robot has already done.

6
00:00:31,519 --> 00:00:37,087
So, rather than treating the reference as a script
to replay, we give the policy two different memories.

7
00:00:37,992 --> 00:00:42,813
First, we need a reference. That can be
a human demonstration, supplied directly.

8
00:00:42,953 --> 00:00:49,182
Or we can search a bank of robot demonstrations,
using only the initial image and the instruction.

9
00:00:49,322 --> 00:00:56,789
The search never sees future frames from the current task.
And finding a reference isn't the same as proving it works in control.

10
00:00:56,929 --> 00:01:01,281
We haven't reported policy results for
the retrieved-reference configuration yet.

11
00:01:02,158 --> 00:01:08,985
Here's how the current design reads a video. We start with
one reference segment, not a sequence of separate segments.

12
00:01:09,125 --> 00:01:12,688
From that same segment, we sample two complementary views.

13
00:01:12,828 --> 00:01:18,886
Sparse frames give us semantic context: the objects,
the scene, and what the operation is about.

14
00:01:19,026 --> 00:01:24,146
A dense slice of consecutive frames preserves
the local movement between those moments.

15
00:01:25,033 --> 00:01:32,649
The semantic frames go through the Qwen3-VL vision tower.
The motion slice goes through the V-JEPA2 encoder.

16
00:01:32,789 --> 00:01:37,995
Both encoders stay frozen. Their outputs
are tokens, shown here as small squares.

17
00:01:38,135 --> 00:01:42,529
Separate Q-formers gather those tokens into compact representations.

18
00:01:42,669 --> 00:01:49,645
We then use semantics as the base, adding motion through
a gated residual. That becomes the reference context.

19
00:01:50,533 --> 00:01:54,885
Now, how do we remember that context?
We don't just put the video in a cache.

20
00:01:55,025 --> 00:02:01,575
Context produces keys and values, which update a small
fast-weight learner through an inner learning objective.

21
00:02:01,715 --> 00:02:06,387
The action stream supplies queries.
It reads the memory, but doesn't write to it.

22
00:02:06,527 --> 00:02:10,815
So test-time learning changes a small state, not the whole policy network.

23
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:15,817
The purple memory stores the reference before rollout, then stays fixed.

24
00:02:15,957 --> 00:02:21,120
The pink memory has a different job.
It waits until an actual transition has finished.

25
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:27,361
Only then do we write the new observation, the robot
state, and the action that was really executed.

26
00:02:27,501 --> 00:02:33,133
As interaction continues, progress keeps
changing. The original intent stays protected.

27
00:02:34,033 --> 00:02:37,041
When choosing an action, the order of the reads matters.

28
00:02:37,181 --> 00:02:44,456
Each action block first reads the reference. Progress then
refines that reference-conditioned action representation.

29
00:02:44,596 --> 00:02:49,183
Notice what changes: the action representation, not the reference memory.

30
00:02:49,323 --> 00:02:56,747
During denoising, both memories are read-only. And before a
memory has been written, its dynamic contribution is zero.

31
00:02:57,658 --> 00:03:07,216
What do the experiments show? At the historical 240,000-update
checkpoint, we evaluated 50 tasks across four difficulty levels.

32
00:03:07,356 --> 00:03:16,700
With human references, RoboDemo reached first success
in 1,692 of 2,000 trials. That's 84.6 percent.

33
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:21,661
The aggregate is higher than the archived DP and ACT baselines shown here.

34
00:03:21,801 --> 00:03:24,852
That doesn't mean we're best on every individual task.

35
00:03:25,742 --> 00:03:31,160
These are recorded policy executions in
simulation, not generated robot footage.

36
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:37,060
Across six selected cases, all six succeeded
with a reference, and none succeeded without one.

37
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:42,448
That's useful evidence of sensitivity to the
input, but it's not a full-benchmark ablation.

38
00:03:42,588 --> 00:03:50,354
Some static and reversed references also succeeded. So these
examples don't prove that the complete temporal order is essential.

39
00:03:51,242 --> 00:03:59,028
We also look at the same 309 human clips in two feature
spaces: appearance on the left, fast weights on the right.

40
00:03:59,168 --> 00:04:06,358
The saved projections show task structure. But a visible
cluster isn't, by itself, proof of action understanding.

41
00:04:06,498 --> 00:04:11,959
And evidence for general alignment between
human and robot demonstrations is still limited.

42
00:04:12,867 --> 00:04:21,101
There's one important distinction to keep in mind. The current
method uses a single segment. The historical checkpoint used four.

43
00:04:21,241 --> 00:04:25,828
Those earlier results don't automatically validate the revised architecture.

44
00:04:25,968 --> 00:04:33,947
And 84.6 percent measures whether a trial ever succeeded,
not whether it was successful at the final, five-hundredth step.

45
00:04:34,825 --> 00:04:43,017
The takeaway is simple. A robot shouldn't have to choose between
following the demonstration and responding to what actually happened.

46
00:04:43,157 --> 00:04:49,472
RoboDemo keeps both kinds of context: the intended
procedure, and the experience of carrying it out.

47
00:04:49,612 --> 00:04:52,662
Two memories, read in order, to guide the next action.
